WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.200
On Conflict Zone this week: Ethiopia's&nbsp;
Civil War, now more than a year old and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.840
showing no sign of ending. All sides&nbsp;
are accused of unspeakable atrocities.&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.920
Zadig Abraha is the Minister for Democratization&nbsp;
and he joins me from the capital Addis Ababa.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.600
What we want is peace. We have&nbsp;
been trying to secure peace.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.240
It turns out your troops have committed&nbsp;
just as many war crimes as the other side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.720
Are they ordered to do that or are&nbsp;
they out of control? Which is it?

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.840
No, we aim at holding our troops&nbsp;
to heighten their standards.&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.560
The country's Prime Minister, Abiy Ahmed, won&nbsp;
a Nobel Prize for making peace with neighboring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.840
Eritrea. This time, he's promised to bury&nbsp;
his enemies in the northern region of Tigray.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.760
While they fight more than two&nbsp;
million people have been displaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.280
and thousands face famine.&nbsp;
Will Ethiopia's government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:01.200
stop the mass detention of Tigrayans? Will&nbsp;
it end the humanitarian blockade on Tigray?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:11.840
And how does the government justify its crackdown&nbsp;
on the media? All that and more on Conflict Zone.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.800
Zadig Abraha, welcome to Conflict Zone.&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.360
Good to see you Tim, same here.
Your government has promised to hold accountable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:29.920
any of your forces that violated human rights,&nbsp;
especially those who committed acts of rape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.160
How many of your soldiers have been&nbsp;
tried and sentenced for these violations?&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.160
I cannot come up with a specific number because it&nbsp;
is an ongoing issue. The investigation has not yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:47.280
stopped and since we have an ongoing conflict...
Well, has it started? You say it hasn't stopped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.760
has it started?
Yes, it has.&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.360
So people have been sentenced, have they?
It is — a number of people have been brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:01.840
to a court of law, so the sentence has yet&nbsp;
to happen, but still the investigation is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.560
ongoing one. More people, we believe more and&nbsp;
more people will be answerable to this process.&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.160
So nobody has been sentenced so&nbsp;
far, is what you're telling me.&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.400
No, what I'm saying and what I'm&nbsp;
telling you is it's up to the courts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.080
The government has committed itself and has&nbsp;
organized the committee overseeing this process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.160
And the government has also exposed itself&nbsp;
to scrutiny of comprising the UNHCR, the UN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.360
Human Rights Commission. So when it comes to...
I understand, I wasn't asking you about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.800
process. I was asking you about&nbsp;
the result, whether anybody has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.640
actually been sentenced so far.
I do not have a specific number now.&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.280
I'm not going. Has anybody been sentenced? I&nbsp;
don't even need the number. Has anybody been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.880
sentenced so far?
Yes.&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.240
And are we talking about five,&nbsp;
10, 50? Roughly how many?&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.440
I cannot come up with a specific number.
And can you tell me what the range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.760
penalties has been so far?
Still, I don't have a number.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.400
I don't have a specific information as to&nbsp;
that, but it is important, that we started...&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.560
This isn't a very transparent&nbsp;
process, is it Mr Abraha?&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.400
Is it a very transparent process.
Well, it isn't if you can't even tell me how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:20.640
people have been arrested, detained, prosecuted...
It doesn't mean ... I don't have a number now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.240
but it doesn't mean this is not a transparent&nbsp;
process. We have shown our commitment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:33.200
transparency to accountability, by subjecting&nbsp;
ourselves to a huge scrutiny. So I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.880
transparency is an issue here. It's just a&nbsp;
matter of information, and I don't have it now.&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:40.160
Well, it is, according to the Office&nbsp;
of the Human Rights Commissioner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.080
She flagged up considerable concerns about your&nbsp;
judicial processes. She said, disturbingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.520
that your court proceedings lacked transparency&nbsp;
and did not meet international standards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.000
What's your excuse for that? You told me&nbsp;
there's transparency, she says there isn't any.&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.000
We have introduced a series of reforms&nbsp;
with a view to making our courts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.960
up the international standard, to making them&nbsp;
transparent ones. And we have a gained, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.960
gone a number of steps when it comes to that. So&nbsp;
I think one of the things where we can proudly say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.120
when it comes to our judicial processes&nbsp;
for the past three and a half years we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.560
have introduced a number of reforms and&nbsp;
these reforms have yielded transparency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:23.760
and accountability. And if you were here to see&nbsp;
it in person, I am sure you wouldn't say that.&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.320
Why don't — Why don't these trials take place&nbsp;
in public with the press free to report on them?&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:37.040
You know, some of these things are, can only&nbsp;
be presided by the courts, the military courts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:45.120
and when it's, if it happens to be civilians,&nbsp;
it has to be seen by a, by a civilian court. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.520
we have no problem with making it transparent.&nbsp;
It's a matter of I never heard the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:58.400
media having an interest in these things.
The media are very interested because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.680
not only important that justice is done, justice&nbsp;
has to be seen to be done as well, doesn't it.&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.480
This government has shown a high level of&nbsp;
commitment to transparency and accountability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.880
time and again, and making it transparent&nbsp;
to a media, it's up to the courts.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.800
A joint report by the UN Human Rights Office&nbsp;
and Ethiopia's Human Rights Commission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.400
was released last month. It turns out your&nbsp;
troops have committed just as many war crimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:32.800
as the other side. Are they ordered to do&nbsp;
that or are they out of control? Which is it?&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:40.240
No, we aim at holding our troops to&nbsp;
high ethical standards. And you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.360
you have the number of reservations&nbsp;
with regard to the outcome of this…&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:52.000
You accepted the report. You accepted the report.
Yeah, but we have reservations about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.680
We have not only accepted this outcome, we&nbsp;
have started implementing the recommendations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:04.640
indicated by the joint investigation, by&nbsp;
establishing a joint ministerial committee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:12.080
So our army, when you talk of our army we&nbsp;
believe, it exhibits, largely exhibited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:19.920
ethics and discipline. But there are some&nbsp;
outlets that are, that are gone out of the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:25.120
that have committed other various acts or these&nbsp;
things, atrocities indicated in the report.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:31.440
And we are committed to make sure that these&nbsp;
individuals are brought to a court of law and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.440
get their sentences as per the law of the nation.
Not only have atrocities been committed minister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:44.640
but Michelle Bachelet, the UN chief human rights&nbsp;
officer, says it appears your forces, along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:50.480
those of Eritrea, committed the majority of&nbsp;
the violations for the first eight months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:56.000
of the conflict between November 2020 and&nbsp;
June this year. That's a hugely damaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.800
observation for your government, isn't it?
It is regrettable that these atrocities happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.640
We have differences when it comes to the number&nbsp;
of atrocities committed and who committed it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.640
But with all that, we don't want to debate on the&nbsp;
amount and the intensity of the forces. Whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.160
atrocities may have happened on one person, on&nbsp;
two persons, it doesn't matter. We don't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:23.600
tolerate it. We have zero tolerance to this kind&nbsp;
of atrocities. But if you want to know the larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:29.680
picture of our army, our army has been throughout&nbsp;
a number of African countries, it is an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:36.080
of discipline and effectiveness, and that does&nbsp;
not represent the larger picture of our army.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:43.040
But these are individuals that are being brought&nbsp;
to a court of law. And we will continue to do so.&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:50.080
You, you say your army adheres to, to high&nbsp;
standards, but there are a remarkable variety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:58.480
a huge variety of serious crimes listed&nbsp;
as having been committed by your forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:04.800
attacks against civilians and indiscriminate&nbsp;
attacks, for instance. November 28 last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:10.560
the report says, your army shelled civilian&nbsp;
facilities in Mekelle, killing at least 29&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:16.640
people and injuring 34. Doesn't that shock you?
I said it's very regrettable and we don't tolerate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:24.320
this kind of action, these kind of violations.&nbsp;
But it doesn't change the fact that our army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:31.280
has been chosen to be a member of the UN peace&nbsp;
keeping mission throughout Africa. And it is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.720
without a reason. It's because of its exhibit&nbsp;
of discipline and ethics that it was chosen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:44.320
to be to oversee this kind of missions. But&nbsp;
still in Tigray, where our army was in Tigray,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:51.040
the larger picture, the bigger picture is, our&nbsp;
army has been supplying the people with food, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.320
fuel ... our army has been apportioning from...
And I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:02.400
I want to come on to the question of aid a bit&nbsp;
later. But before we leave these crimes, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:08.880
of the categories highlighted by the report&nbsp;
was sexual and gender-based violence. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:14.960
the report says your army, along with&nbsp;
other forces, was implicated in multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:22.240
reports of gang rape used to degrade and&nbsp;
dehumanize victims. So these crimes — multiple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.440
multiple reports of gang rape. These crimes&nbsp;
aren't just the work of a few rogue soldiers, are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.760
they, as some of your colleagues have suggested?&nbsp;
They're systematic, they're practiced, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:37.200
they're repetitive. I wonder what would lead your&nbsp;
troops to think those violations are acceptable?&nbsp;

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:42.560
There is no way. For one thing, I have said that&nbsp;
we have accepted this report with reservations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:47.840
And there is no way for any kind of&nbsp;
rape to be systematic here in Ethiopia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:53.360
Tigrayian people are our people. Our army was&nbsp;
there to liberate Tigrayan people from the yoke of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:59.920
the TPLF, from the authoritarian hold of the TPLF.&nbsp;
And there is no way for this to be systematic. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:06.720
is only the act of individuals within the army and&nbsp;
we are making sure that these people are punished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:13.760
for their criminal deeds. It does not explain...&nbsp;
It does not represent the act of our army. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:20.560
one can possibly say that this is systematic. Our&nbsp;
system, our, — if you want — if it was systematic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.240
there was, there was no possibility for this&nbsp;
people to be brought in the court of law.&nbsp;

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:30.320
We now know, Minister, we now know, Minister,&nbsp;
that terrible crimes were also committed by your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:36.480
allies from Eritrea. You told this program last&nbsp;
December that you were a thousand percent sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:40.560
that there were no Eritrean troops in your&nbsp;
country. In fact, they'd been there since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:45.840
last November, according to a Reuters special&nbsp;
investigation. Why? Why didn't you mention that?&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:53.760
I said, I talked the Eritrean army right&nbsp;
now, underline right now. And for second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:57.600
you know, we might have differences …
But the interview was in December and the Eritrean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.400
troops were there in November, months before.
You know, when it comes to transparency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:08.080
when the day, when the time came, when,&nbsp;
when we believe when our intel showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:13.280
the Eritrean army was there, the prime minister&nbsp;
has come up to the parliament and talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:19.760
it. And then, you know, we may have differences&nbsp;
on intel. Your information may show you September,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:25.920
mine was right before, after December. That's&nbsp;
why. It was not said out of an intention to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.960
mislead international community. It was said&nbsp;
based on the information we have on the ground.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:36.560
But it wasn't until March that Prime Minister&nbsp;
Abiy revealed that Eritrean forces had entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:43.040
Ethiopia, he said, to support him. Up until then,&nbsp;
your government was dismissing all the reports as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:50.000
hostile propaganda. And in April, your Information&nbsp;
Minister Yemane Gebremeskel said the reports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:57.760
about Eritrean troops raping Tigrayan women&nbsp;
were simply fabricated stories, fabricated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:02.960
by the party in power in Tigray, the Tigrayan&nbsp;
People's Liberation Front. That was nonsense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:07.520
The stories were not fabricated. They did rape&nbsp;
people and they raped a lot of women, didn't they?&nbsp;

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:16.160
Well, I think the right person... I'm here&nbsp;
to say, when the information we had on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:21.040
ground was confirmed, we have come up&nbsp;
in public and talked about it. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:29.200
we lacked information and we come, appeared&nbsp;
on Conflict Zone and came up with and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:38.320
talked about my take of the situation. That's all.
In May, your attorney general admitted that your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:45.920
Eritrean friends had executed 110 civilians&nbsp;
in cold blood in a place called Aksum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:54.400
What do you think of your Eritrean friends now?
You know, following the reform introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.520 --> 00:13:00.000
in the country, we have come&nbsp;
up with the following points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.480
that mainly gives priority to our neighbors.&nbsp;
That has changed the situation. Ethiopian...&nbsp;

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:11.680
110 civilians murdered in cold&nbsp;
blood. Are these people going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:17.463
to be held responsible by your government?
Yes, you know, we have a good relation with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.463 --> 00:13:23.120
Eritrea. We believe the Eritrean government&nbsp;
will also be part of this effort to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:29.920
these people to justice, and we are not here…
You are going to prosecute Eritrean soldiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:34.960
are you? Are you telling me now your government&nbsp;
intends to prosecute Eritrean soldiers.&nbsp;

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:39.600
As we have indicated, whatever&nbsp;
atrocities committed by whoever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:45.520
it will be prosecuted. Where it, who... where …&nbsp;
Eritrea, Ethiopia or is it anywhere different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:51.840
that's another story. What I can confirm you - no&nbsp;
kind of human rights violations will be tolerated.&nbsp;

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:55.840
Are Eritrean forces still in Ethiopia?&nbsp;
Your prime minister said in March that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:59.600
he'd got an agreement for them to leave,&nbsp;
but they haven't left yet, have they?&nbsp;

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:02.720
They have left the country.
When did they leave?&nbsp;

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:08.000
Well that specific time and day has...&nbsp;

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:12.880
Can you give me a month? Can you can you say&nbsp;
whether it was a month ago, three months ago ...&nbsp;

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:19.840
I don't want to be that specific, but I can assure&nbsp;
you that Eritrean forces have left the country.&nbsp;

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:24.320
And you say that with the same kind of&nbsp;
authority as you said, when a year ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:29.840
when you said they weren't in the country?
Well, based on the information I had on my hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:35.200
I have told you that now is the&nbsp;
information, we have intel we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:39.440
on our hand shows that and I am here to tell you.
Mr. Minister, part of the reason for a lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.600
of transparency in Ethiopia these&nbsp;
days is your crackdown on the press,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:49.840
which is something that you vehemently denied&nbsp;
on this program a year ago. Your denials haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:54.480
held up very well, have they? The Committee to&nbsp;
Protect Journalists said a week ago that since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:59.920
the start of the war, they've documented multiple&nbsp;
violations of press freedom by your government.

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:08.080
We have got rid of a draconian press law and&nbsp;
came up with a new progressive press law. We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:14.880
introduced, we have the, following this reform,&nbsp;
the press freedom in Ethiopia has increased in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:19.040
both in terms of numbers and quality. I'm telling&nbsp;
you, it's something that speaks for itself.&nbsp;

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:24.400
On December the 1st, at least 9 reporters&nbsp;
were in custody. If they've committed crimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:27.920
charge them if they haven't let them go.
Who are these 9 reporters, can you, can you, can -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:29.920
because as far as my information, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:38.160
the, the problem is, our definition of&nbsp;
journalism is different. There are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:47.280
social media activists that are working towards&nbsp;
hampering a rule of law, trying to cause havoc.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:57.440
At the end of October, you forced the closure&nbsp;
of the Awlo Media Centre, an online news outlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:02.080
that was critical of the prime minister. Before&nbsp;
that, the Committee to Protect Journalists says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:09.520
media workers were arrested in late June and&nbsp;
detained incommunicado for several weeks at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:15.360
a military camp in the east of the country.&nbsp;
You detain these people incommunicado. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:22.400
mafia-style thuggery by your government, isn't it?
Let me tell you, there are journalists that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:28.960
committing crime. And if that is the case, we&nbsp;
will continue to make sure that even when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:35.120
comes to journalists, their criminal deeds&nbsp;
are punished. But we have, we have seen for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:41.360
the past four years, we have stopped press&nbsp;
freedom in the cause for journalists to be...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:47.760
We have, that era is gone. This report has to be&nbsp;
cross-checked. I have to come up. I have to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:51.440
the names of specific names, specific information&nbsp;
because you are coming up with a number that is...&nbsp;

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:59.600
I give you, I'll give you specific information&nbsp;
minister. Court orders for the release of two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:06.320
journalists were given. At least two cases&nbsp;
where court orders were given to release them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:13.600
from custody. And they weren't released. Kibrom&nbsp;
Worku, a radio journalist and Tesfa-Alem Tekle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:17.840
correspondent for Nation Media Group. They&nbsp;
were still in detention earlier this month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:23.840
weeks after they were granted bail. Now you&nbsp;
present the picture of a government that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:29.360
ignores the rulings of its own courts.&nbsp;
That's the lawless country, Minister.&nbsp;

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:34.240
You know, what happened is the public&nbsp;
prosecutor office appealed to the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:39.760
level of court and when, when an appeal is&nbsp;
being heard, you cannot free a journalist.&nbsp;

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:44.960
You know, Minster, that you that your own Human&nbsp;
Rights Commission has also expressed grave concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:50.480
about the detention of thousands of people&nbsp;
in the capital under your state of emergency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:55.760
and the difficulty of gathering information&nbsp;
about the treatment of those in detention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.760 --> 00:18:02.000
Are they wrong, too? Are they wrong, too?
We are aware of that and we have, we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:06.960
working day and night to correct that problem.
You are aware of it? You are in charge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.280
either you are in charge of the country or not,&nbsp;
you're ordering these detentions. No, it's not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:17.200
That is not enough to be aware of them, is it.
No, no. This is a practice of certain police and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:22.400
we are, we are, we are in charge. We are&nbsp;
correcting these mistakes. The thing is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:26.320
we have introduced a state of emergency.&nbsp;
And following that, there are people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:33.440
who are suspected of committing crimes and they&nbsp;
are under the rule of law that they are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:38.000
prosecuted. And in this process that… there are&nbsp;
wrongs here and there and we are correcting them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:43.600
You know, the result why, in our own human&nbsp;
rights commission have become stronger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:49.680
It is coming up, it is issuing these kind of&nbsp;
reports, which is exposing even our wrongs. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:53.360
is a result of the transparency, the result&nbsp;
of accountability measures we have taken.&nbsp;

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:59.200
You've also taken measures against senior&nbsp;
UN officials. You threw out seven of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:04.320
in September. You didn't like them telling the&nbsp;
truth. The expulsions were ordered two days after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:09.840
the UN's aid chief warned that your restrictions&nbsp;
on the flow of aid had probably forced hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:15.360
of thousands of people in Tigray into famine. Do&nbsp;
you throw out the foreigners now because you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:19.840
like what they're saying about your government?
Not, not at all. We believe in working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:27.200
the UN. It is a good partner. We are a&nbsp;
member of the UN. So we have, we will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:33.680
we will work with the UN under any circumstances.&nbsp;
But it doesn't mean that these individuals have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:41.680
… their own code of conduct, their own, their own&nbsp;
bylaws and there were interfering in our internal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:49.040
issues, but that measure was taken following that.&nbsp;
And the government has explained the reason why we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:54.240
have taken that measure at that point in time.
The White House in Washington, says that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.240 --> 00:20:00.960
have exhibited a pattern of obstructing the&nbsp;
delivery of food, medicine and other lifesaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:06.960
supplies to those who are most in need. I&nbsp;
presume you are aware that blocking food and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:12.320
medical supplies without justification violates&nbsp;
international humanitarian law. Or don't you care?&nbsp;

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:15.200
That's wrong, that's wrong.&nbsp;
That's not what we are doing.

00:20:15.200 --> 00:20:22.000
Minister, this war is looking increasingly&nbsp;
unwinnable. Will you negotiate or are you only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:26.400
interested in outright victory?
You know, we have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:32.320
we have a lot to gain in peace, and we&nbsp;
have a lot to lose in the absence of peace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:40.160
So what we want is peace. We have been trying&nbsp;
to secure peace, through informal, informal …,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:44.560
but TPLF gave all these efforts a&nbsp;
deaf ear. That's why we are here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:53.280
And, you know, we have to wait a couple of&nbsp;
weeks for these things to unravel themselves.&nbsp;

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:59.200
Minister, the Joint Investigative Report&nbsp;
issued last month, calls on you and the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:05.200
combatants to immediately end hostilities&nbsp;
without preconditions and end any measures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:10.160
that are exacerbating the already acute&nbsp;
humanitarian crisis. Will you do that?&nbsp;

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:19.760
We, on June, we, we declared a unilateral&nbsp;
cease-fire in the … from Tigray. But TPLF said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:26.480
it was a sick joke. That's how our, our move for&nbsp;
peace, our initiation for peace, was welcomed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:29.600
You know, we have a duty to protect our citizens.

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:36.400
You've all done unspeakably awful things to&nbsp;
each other. How can there be reconciliation now?&nbsp;

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:43.200
We have no problems in reconciliation. We have&nbsp;
no problem with peace. The TPLF has to go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:49.440
to where it was. Now it is occupying a number of&nbsp;
cities in Amhara region and committing atrocities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:53.360
That has to be stopped. We have a duty to protect&nbsp;
our citizens out there they are crying for help.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:58.320
Your forces have committed&nbsp;
atrocities as well, let's be honest.

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:02.400
You know, we take seriously&nbsp;
any kind of allegations, we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:05.600
looking into that, we have taken a&nbsp;
number of measures to correct that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:13.680
But what is TPLF doing? You know, if you happen&nbsp;
to go to a number of cities in Amhara region, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:21.600
know, cities are destroyed to ground zero. Women&nbsp;
are raped, farmers are robbed of their yields.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:27.440
Again, your forces have also been implicated&nbsp;
in rapes. You seem to be very selective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:30.474
you seem to be very selective in accusing the TPLF

00:22:30.474 --> 00:22:36.160
No, the difference is, when it comes to us. We are&nbsp;
taking these allegations seriously and taking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:41.680
number of measures to correct it. We are bringing&nbsp;
these perpetrators to a court of law. We are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:48.400
ensuring accountability and transparency. But TPLF&nbsp;
is systematically, systematically doing that …

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:53.040
Minister, why did you reject a plan by the UN&nbsp;
Human Rights Council to discuss what it called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:57.760
the grave human rights situation in your&nbsp;
country, in Geneva on December the 17th?

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:03.760
More than 50-plus countries now called&nbsp;
on discussing the issue. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:11.840
no African country is party to such move. It&nbsp;
is, and in African countries, discussing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:16.400
African issue. And the second thing is we have&nbsp;
accepted the outcome of the Joint Committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:22.080
and we have also started to take measures. We&nbsp;
have started to implement the recommendations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:28.560
by forming inter-ministerial committee. And that’s&nbsp;
nonsense when we have taken these steps. Why?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:31.040
Why now? And the third thing is, TPLF&nbsp;
is committing a number of violations…

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:35.840
Question is why not? And&nbsp;
what have you got to hide?

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:41.120
… in Amhara region, in Afar region&nbsp;
and this has been given a deaf ear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:47.840
This is not, you know, any human rights&nbsp;
violation committed by our forces or TPLF&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:56.400
it has to be taken seriously. It has to be put&nbsp;
on par and investigated without discrimination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.400 --> 00:24:02.000
without arrogance. We don't see this happening.&nbsp;
You know, if, if, if there is another, there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:07.200
this allegation, committed by our forces&nbsp;
and we see things being … Why do you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:13.520
other African countries have joined that move,&nbsp;
apart of that signature. Why do you think?

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:16.080
You tell me.

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:20.800
Because African countries understand that we&nbsp;
have accepted the outcome of the Joint Committee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:25.040
the investigation of the Joint Committee. And we&nbsp;
have taken, we have started to take the necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:32.400
measures. Where it is … and at the same time other&nbsp;
African countries are aware of the fact that TPLF&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:38.560
is committing a number of human rights violations&nbsp;
in a number of Amhara cities. And where they see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:43.200
the situation, it's a double standard. And as&nbsp;
a result they are not part of this signature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.160 --> 00:24:49.760
So this is not something where we are the only&nbsp;
country against this move. A number of African&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:52.880
countries are against this move. That's&nbsp;
why they are not part of this initiative.

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:58.880
You've called on everybody to reject this meeting.&nbsp;
Looks like you have something to hide, Minister.&nbsp;

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:05.920
No, it's not. If we had something to hide, we&nbsp;
wouldn't have subjected ourselves to the scrutiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:10.800
of the Joint Committee by UNHCR and our own human&nbsp;
rights commission. That wouldn't have happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.760 --> 00:25:14.880
We, you know, we have allowed&nbsp;
them to come on ground and check.

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:20.720
Alright, Minister Zadig Abraha, I am&nbsp;
afraid we're out of time. Thank you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:23.520
very much for being on Conflict&nbsp;
Zone. Thank you for your time.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:27.840
Thank you very much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.760 --> 00:26:00.880
Same here.
Thank you.

